{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>HaShem, HaShem, El, compassionate.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Rosh HaShanah 17b) that these two are attributes of mercy — one before a person sins and one after he sins. And the Rosh of blessed memory raised the difficulty: before he sins, why is compassion necessary? And his answer is that compassion is necessary because HaShem knows that he is destined to sin; or the verse speaks of one contemplating idolatrous worship and calling upon Him before he sins — meaning before he transgresses. But his words, both the first and the last, are not clear to me, for if HaShem were to come to punish for what is destined, we could say before Him as well: why did You create him, since You know he will sin? And He would judge him for the future, for before his birth and before his sin the case would be the same, and even close to the act of sin itself — and understand this. And what he wrote about one contemplating idolatrous worship who has not yet acted — this is difficult, for this is called after the fact, for even though he did not worship, the sin of thought is held against him." "And I say that one needs compassion even before he sins: if he has no merit that is the fulfillment of positive commandments to benefit from them, the Master grants him compassion. And behold, before you is the verse (Deuteronomy 11:13): ״וְהָיָה אִם שָׁמֹעַ תִּשְׁמְעוּ״ וגו׳ ״וְנָתַתִּי מְטַר״ וגו׳ — see that in reward for good deed, good comes; and if a person were constrained and did not learn, etc., he has neither reward nor punishment, and by what shall he be shown compassion? Therefore HaShem said: before he sins — and this is a novelty. Further, even if his deeds turned ugly and he transgressed negative commandments, He conducts Himself toward him with compassion." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to inform us that when HaShem in His goodness does good to a man by way of compassion, even though he sinned, He does not diminish His good at all, and the attribute that was active beforehand continues to function even after he sinned. Therefore He said רַחוּם — compassion — to equate the compassion that exists before he sins with the compassion that exists after he sins. And with this my mind was set at rest upon seeing that the good enjoyed by the wicked does not change or diminish, for such is the attribute."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:39.374376628Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}